            "   Recession coefficient: Blue=-0.0464, White=-0.0305\n",
            "\n",
            "2. Slower recovery: BLUE-COLLAR jobs\n",
            "   Avg recovery: Blue=57.5 months, White=23.4 months\n",
            "\n",
            "3. Model R² values show how much of future job loss the recession indicator + lags explain.\n",
            "   Higher R² = more predictable. Lower R² = more idiosyncratic shocks.\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "### Step 5: Recovery Duration Analysis\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
        "For each distinct recession episode, calculate months to recover pre-recession peak\n",
        "employment level for blue-collar and white-collar cohorts.\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
        "\n",
        "import warnings\n",
        "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")  # Suppress pandas boolean indexing warnings\n",
        "\n",
        "# Identify distinct recession episodes (contiguous blocks of indicator == 1)\n",
        "recession_episodes = []\n",
        "in_recession = False\n",
        "episode_start = None\n",
        "\n",
        "for date, row in df.iterrows():\n",
        "    if row[\"recession_indicator\"] == 1 and not in_recession:\n",
        "        in_recession = True\n",
        "        episode_start = date\n",
        "    elif row[\"recession_indicator\"] == 0 and in_recession:\n",
        "        in_recession = False\n",
        "        recession_episodes.append((episode_start, date - pd.Timedelta(days=1)))\n",
        "\n",
        "if in_recession:\n",
        "    recession_episodes.append((episode_start, df.index[-1]))\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Distinct recession episodes found: {len(recession_episodes)}\\n\")\n",
        "\n",
        "recovery_rows = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for rec_start, rec_end in recession_episodes:\n",
        "    era = \"Past (1939–1989)\" if rec_start.year <= 1989 else \"Modern (1990–Present)\"\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Base = last observation before recession starts\n",
        "    base_idx = df.index[df.index <= rec_start][-1]\n",
        "    base_blue = df.loc[base_idx, \"blue_collar_jobs\"]\n",
        "    base_white = df.loc[base_idx, \"white_collar_jobs\"]\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Search forward from recession start until employment exceeds base again\n",
        "    post_recession = df.loc[rec_end:]\n",
        "    \n",
        "    blue_recovery_months = None\n",
        "    white_recovery_months = None\n",
        "    \n",
        "    for date, row in post_recession.iterrows():\n",
        "        if blue_recovery_months is None and row[\"blue_collar_jobs\"] >= base_blue:\n",
        "            blue_recovery_months = (date.year - rec_start.year) * 12 + (date.month - rec_start.month)\n",
        "        if white_recovery_months is None and row[\"white_collar_jobs\"] >= base_white:\n",
        "            white_recovery_months = (date.year - rec_start.year) * 12 + (date.month - rec_start.month)\n",
        "        if blue_recovery_months is not None and white_recovery_months is not None:\n",
        "            break\n",
        "    \n",
        "    recovery_rows.append({\n",
        "        \"Era\": era,\n",
        "        \"Recession Start\": rec_start.date(),\n",
        "        \"Cohort\": \"blue_collar\",\n",
        "        \"Recovery Months\": blue_recovery_months\n",
        "    })\n",
        "    recovery_rows.append({\n",
        "        \"Era\": era,\n",
        "        \"Recession Start\": rec_start.date(),\n",
        "        \"Cohort\": \"white_collar\",\n",
        "        \"Recovery Months\": white_recovery_months\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "recovery_df = pd.DataFrame(recovery_rows)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Clean formatted summary: mean recovery by cohort\n",
        "summary = recovery_df.groupby(\"Cohort\")[\"Recovery Months\"].agg([\"mean\", \"min\", \"max\", \"count\"]).reset_index()\n",
        "summary.columns = [\"Cohort\", \"Avg Months\", \"Min Months\", \"Max Months\", \"N Episodes\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"=== RECOVERY DURATION SUMMARY (Months to Return to Pre-Recession Peak) ===\\n\")\n",
        "print(summary.to_string(index=False))\n",
        "print()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Hook #5 answers\n",
        "blue_coef = results_df[results_df[\"cohort\"] == \"blue_collar_jobs\"][\"recession_coef\"].iloc[0]\n",
        "white_coef = results_df[results_df[\"cohort\"] == \"white_collar_jobs\"][\"recession_coef\"].iloc[0]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"=== HOOK #5 ANSWERS ===\\n\")\n",
        "if blue_coef < white_coef:\n",
        "    print(\"1. Steeper wipeout: BLUE-COLLAR jobs\")\n",
        "    print(f\"   Recession coefficient: Blue={blue_coef:.4f}, White={white_coef:.4f}\\n\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print(\"1. Steeper wipeout: WHITE-COLLAR jobs\")\n",
        "    print(f\"   Recession coefficient: White={white_coef:.4f}, Blue={blue_coef:.4f}\\n\")\n",
        "\n",
        "avg_rec_blue = summary[summary[\"Cohort\"] == \"blue_collar\"][\"Avg Months\"].iloc[0]\n",
        "avg_rec_white = summary[summary[\"Cohort\"] == \"white_collar\"][\"Avg Months\"].iloc[0]\n",
        "\n",
        "if pd.notna(avg_rec_blue) and pd.notna(avg_rec_white):\n",
        "    if avg_rec_blue > avg_rec_white:\n",
        "        print(\"2. Slower recovery: BLUE-COLLAR jobs\")\n",
        "        print(f\"   Avg recovery: Blue={avg_rec_blue:.1f} months, White={avg_rec_white:.1f} months\\n\")\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        print(\"2. Slower recovery: WHITE-COLLAR jobs\")\n",
        "        print(f\"   Avg recovery: White={avg_rec_white:.1f} months, Blue={avg_rec_blue:.1f} months\\n\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"3. Model R² values show how much of future job loss the recession indicator + lags explain.\")\n",
        "print(\"   Higher R² = more predictable. Lower R² = more idiosyncratic shocks.\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Step 6: Final Conclusion — Hook #5 Answer\n",
        "\n",
        "**The linear regression model fails out-of-sample.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The model achieves moderate fit on historical training data (Train R²: 0.42 blue-collar, 0.34 white-collar) but produces **negative out-of-sample test R²** when evaluated on unseen data that includes the 2020 COVID-19 shock:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Cohort | Out-of-Sample Test R² | Train R² | Recession Coefficient |\n",
        "|--------|----------------------|----------|----------------------|\n",
        "| Blue-Collar | -4.95 | 0.42 | -0.0464 |\n",
        "| White-Collar | -0.76 | 0.34 | -0.0305 |\n",
        "\n",
        "**What this means:**\n",
        "Linear models trained on historical trends (1939–2019) cannot reliably predict job losses during modern black-swan shocks. The 2020 COVID crash was structurally different from every prior recession in the training window, making forward-looking linear extrapolation ineffective.\n",
        "\n",
        "**What we CAN say with confidence:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Finding | Evidence |\n",
        "|---------|----------|\n",
        "| **Blue-collar jobs suffer steeper immediate wipeouts** | Recession coefficient: Blue = -0.0464 vs White = -0.0305 |\n",
        "| **Blue-collar jobs recover ~2.5x slower** | Avg recovery: Blue ≈ 57.5 months vs White ≈ 23.4 months |\n",
        "| **Modern recessions break linear models** | Out-of-sample R² is negative for both cohorts |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Recovery Episode Details:**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Cohort | Avg Months | Min Months | Max Months | N Episodes |\n",
        "|--------|-----------|------------|------------|------------|\n",
        "| Blue-Collar | 57.5 | 19 | 226 | 11 |\n",
        "| White-Collar | 23.4 | 7 | 62 | 13 |\n",
        "\n",
        "*Note: Blue-Collar employment never fully recovered to its pre-recession peak before the next downturn in 2 episodes (e.g., following the 2001 and 2008 recessions).*\n",
        "\n",
        "Total distinct recession episodes analyzed: 13\n",
        "\n",
